-- Curated, human-reviewed self-test — same set served at every level.
with q as (
  select $l19_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter calls a human 'a payload with a payload,' with a ratio of about a hundred to one. What does that mean?", "options": ["A human weighs a hundred times more than a robot", "An ~80 kg human requires ~10,000+ kg of machine \u2014 pressurised habitat, life support, consumables, shielding, and a return vehicle \u2014 built solely to keep it alive, so the life-sustaining apparatus dwarfs the human itself", "A crew is always a hundred people", "Humans need a hundred times more fuel per kilogram than cargo"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "You are not launching an 80 kg person; you are launching a person plus a complete portable fragment of Earth \u2014 a sealed pressurised vessel, CO2 scrubbers and oxygen generators and water recyclers, radiation shielding, consumables, and the vehicle and propellant to come home. That apparatus masses on the order of ten thousand kilograms, so the human sits at the centre of a ~100:1 ratio of supporting machine to body. Contrast Curiosity (nearly all instrument) or Opportunity (90-day design, 5,111 days worked, no ride home): a robot is a payload, a human is a payload with a payload."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "A human needs ~5 kg of consumables per day. Why does this make long missions so much harder than short ones, in a way a robot's power needs do not?", "options": ["Consumables are radioactive and decay", "Consumables are continuously CONSUMED, so the required mass scales with crew x days and grows without bound with duration (a Mars crew of four needs ~20 tonnes), whereas a robot's power is a fixed one-time mass (a solar panel) that supplies energy indefinitely without growing", "Food gets heavier in space", "Robots don't need any power"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Per person per day: ~0.84 kg oxygen, ~3.5 kg water, ~1.8 kg food, rolled to ~5 kg with packaging. Because this is consumed every day, the total is crew x days x 5 kg, and duration is the enemy: 3 crew x 10 days = 150 kg, but 4 crew x 1000 days = 20,000 kg = 20 tonnes, before anything else. A robot's consumables are just electrical power \u2014 a fixed one-time mass of solar panel or nuclear source that never grows with time. The human must be fed a continuous supply chain of physical matter, which is why long missions must recycle."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does the requirement to RETURN the crew multiply a human mission's mass so severely?", "options": ["Return doubles the mass, since you fly the trip twice", "You must carry the return propellant OUT with you, and by the rocket equation each leg's mass ratio (e^(dv/ve)) multiplies the ones before it \u2014 so a single leg of ratio ~3 can compound to ~10 on the return stack; every kilogram home costs tens (Moon) to hundreds (Mars) of kilograms launched", "Returning requires a second, identical rocket", "The crew gains weight on the journey"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The propellant to launch off Mars must be landed on Mars, which took propellant sent from Earth, which took propellant launched off Earth \u2014 each leg multiplied by the exponential e^(dv/ve). Carrying the return capability out with you compounds the mass ratio (roughly R x R), so the return trip is many times harder, not twice. Hence the rule: every kilogram returned costs many kilograms launched \u2014 worse from Mars (deeper well, longer trip) than the Moon. The escape is in-situ resource utilisation: make the return propellant at the destination instead of dragging it from Earth, collapsing the worst legs of the stack. That one-way missions are even proposed shows how brutal the return penalty is."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Human missions must meet a far higher reliability standard ('human-rating') than robotic ones. What ultimately sets that standard?", "options": ["The scientific value of the mission", "Not the science but the intolerability of killing the crew: when a robot fails you lose a mission, when a human system fails you lose a life \u2014 a moral and political catastrophe \u2014 so redundancy, abort systems, margins, and exhaustive testing are demanded regardless of the mission's scientific worth", "International law requires it", "The rocket equation demands it"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A failed probe is a setback (money and disappointment); a failed crewed mission kills people, and the deaths are public, mourned, and politically consequential \u2014 Apollo 1, Challenger, and Columbia each reshaped the program for years. So human-rating demands redundancy for every critical function, abort/escape systems, generous margins, and exhaustive testing, each adding mass and cost, often doubling a system's price. Crucially the standard is driven by the unacceptability of a dead astronaut, not by what the mission achieves \u2014 you'd demand the same reliability for a scientifically modest crewed flight as a grand one. That is why human missions cost one-to-two orders of magnitude more than robotic ones (Apollo's >$250 billion for twelve Moonwalkers versus a fraction for the robots that explored every planet)."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is a spacesuit described as 'a one-person spacecraft,' and what makes it so hard to use?", "options": ["It is just insulated clothing that keeps the astronaut warm", "It miniaturises the whole portable Earth \u2014 pressure, oxygen, CO2 scrubbing, cooling, power \u2014 around a single body; and because it must hold pressure against vacuum it stiffens into a balloon that fights every movement, so closing a gloved hand means bending a pressurised bag, and the crew must prebreathe oxygen for hours first to avoid the bends", "It lets the astronaut breathe vacuum directly", "It is lighter and simpler than the spacecraft cabin"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A suit provides, around one body, everything the habitat provides around the crew \u2014 pressure, oxygen, CO2 removal, temperature control, water, comms, power \u2014 which is why it is a one-person spacecraft, the portable Earth shrunk to human scale. The same pressure that keeps blood from boiling turns the suit into a stiff balloon: every finger bend fights the inflated bag, so a spacewalk exhausts the forearms and shreds fingernails. And because the suit runs at lower pressure than the cabin, the crew must prebreathe pure oxygen for hours to flush dissolved nitrogen \u2014 otherwise stepping out too fast lets it fizz out of the blood exactly as in a diver surfacing too quickly (the bends). Even to poke one body out of the airlock, you must build it another, smaller spacecraft."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain the central metaphor of the chapter: 'the human is not the payload; the human plus their entire portable biosphere is the payload.' What must this portable Earth provide, why must it be maintained continuously and flawlessly, and how does the metaphor explain why human spaceflight is the hardest thing we do off the Earth?", "rubric": "A human cannot survive raw space for more than seconds to minutes, because the environment is lethal in essentially every parameter at once (vacuum, no oxygen, CO2 buildup, temperature extremes, radiation, no water or food). So to send a human you must send a complete, self-contained fragment of Earth: a sealed pressurised vessel holding the right air at the right pressure; machinery to supply oxygen and continuously scrub the CO2 the crew exhale; temperature and humidity control; water, food, and waste management; radiation shielding; and \u2014 because the destination is lethal and they must survive the trip home \u2014 a vehicle and propellant to return. It must be maintained CONTINUOUSLY and FLAWLESSLY because the human survivable envelope is narrow across every parameter AT ONCE, and failure in any single one (a pressure breach, a scrubber failure, a cooling loss) is injury or death within minutes to days \u2014 there is no pause, no tolerance, no forgiveness, and the payload cannot be turned off. The metaphor reframes the payload: you are not launching an ~80 kg person but a person PLUS the miniaturised life-sustaining apparatus of a planet, and every kilogram of that apparatus (habitat, life support, consumables, shielding, return vehicle) must be paid for exponentially in propellant through the rocket equation and kept perfect for the mission's duration. The human is cheap; the portable Earth that keeps the human alive is what costs the tonnes and the billions \u2014 the essence of why sending people is the hardest thing we do off the Earth."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Human missions cost one to two orders of magnitude more than robotic ones. Explain the THREE compounding burdens that produce this gap, and then explain why the resulting 'why send humans at all?' debate is usually a dispute about values rather than facts.", "rubric": "THE THREE BURDENS, each feeding the rocket equation: (1) MASS \u2014 the human is heavier, because consumables run ~5 kg/person/day and are continuously consumed, ballooning to ~20 tonnes for a Mars crew of four and growing without bound with duration (forcing recycling), and this sits atop a cascade of human-demanded mass (habitat, life support, radiation shielding, safety systems), all multiplied exponentially by Tsiolkovsky. (2) RETURN \u2014 unlike almost every robot, a human must come home, and carrying the means of return out with you compounds through the rocket equation at every stage, so every kilogram returned costs tens (Moon) to hundreds (Mars) of kilograms launched; the return is many times harder than the going. (3) RELIABILITY \u2014 when a human system fails a life is lost, so missions must be human-rated (redundancy, abort systems, margins, exhaustive testing) at a cost set by the intolerability of death, not the science. Together \u2014 heavier, must return, must not fail \u2014 these produce the ~100x cost gap (Apollo >$250 billion for twelve Moonwalkers vs a fraction for the robots that explored every planet). WHY THE DEBATE IS ABOUT VALUES: the skeptic is right that robots do more science per dollar, risk no lives, and reach places humans cannot; the advocate is right that humans bring judgment and real-time adaptability robots can't yet match (field geology, improvised repairs, and Mars's up-to-20-minute light-lag means a rover can't be joysticked while a human acts instantly), generate the knowledge needed for any human future off-Earth, and serve values (exploration, inspiration, multi-planetary insurance) that science-per-dollar doesn't capture. Crucially, both camps often agree on EVERY fact \u2014 humans are more expensive, riskier, and robots return more science per dollar \u2014 and differ only on what spaceflight is FOR: someone optimizing knowledge-per-dollar favours robots, someone optimizing the human future in space favours crews, and both can be entirely rational because they optimize different objectives. It is a values dispute that evidence cannot settle, which is why the sensible response is a portfolio \u2014 robots for the bulk of exploration, humans for the goals that require or are enriched by human presence. A strong answer notes the through-line: never send a human where a machine would do, unless what the human brings is worth the enormous price."}]$l19_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
